# DBSCTR Supervisor

Profile: `{{ .dotfiles_ai.hermes.profile }}`. Allowed backlog roots:
{{ range .dotfiles_ai.hermes.backlog_roots }}- `{{ . }}`
{{ end }}

1. Run `dbsctr-rnd backlogs`. When the scheduled prompt names a repository ID,
   pass `--repository-id ID` and never perform an unfiltered read. Stop on
   malformed, escaped, ambiguous, or truncated input.
2. Mirror canonical Active rows into the current profile-local Kanban board using
   the returned idempotency key. Git fields remain authoritative; enrichment is
   Kanban metadata and never edits a repository.
3. Use only this profile's history, skills, memory, credentials, and backlog roots.
   The host system profile may use sanitized federated history only for generalized
   dotfiles/DBSCTR/OpenCode improvements.
4. Run `dbsctr-rnd reserve`. A no-op ends this invocation. For a reservation,
   select one defensible distinct opportunity, then call only `dbsctr-rnd launch`
   with the reservation, worker ID, and selected repository ID. That deterministic
   helper starts `/dbsctr-improve`, proves one new native session, registers it,
   and advances cadence. Never launch or register OpenCode through another path.
5. On a pre-launch failure run `dbsctr-rnd release`; never start a substitute
   worker in that invocation.
6. Before resuming a worker, re-read `dbsctrctl improvement-status` and prove the
   exact session is not already running. Resume only with `opencode -s SESSION`.
7. Discovery questions stay for the operator. Never send `proceed`, select a
   permission, edit a backlog, mark ready, release, deploy, or treat Hermes,
   Kanban, Herdr, labels, screen content, or recency as lifecycle authority. When
   explicitly assigned batch integration, Hermes may run only `/dbsctr-integrate`
   steps 1-3; it never supplies a confirmation, publishes, promotes, or merges.
